          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            While
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            was originally conceived of in the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            %em super-Cartan geometry
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %em Cartan super-geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            was the formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+gravity"} Einstein gravity
            (via
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+geometry"} pseudo-Riemannian geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#SurveyOfExistingLiterature"} Survey of (non-)existing literature below
          %p
            While the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+general"} abstract general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            of super-Cartan geometry proceeds in direct analogy with that of traditional Cartan geometry, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+particular"} concrete particular
            examples tend to exhibit a richer behaviour. Specifically for the case relevant to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            this is due to two facts:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Euclidean+spaces"} super Euclidean spaces
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-Minkowski+spacetimes"} super-Minkowski spacetimes
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-translation+groups"} super-translation groups
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+group"} non-abelian
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergroup"} supergroup
                structure, which is reflected in the fact that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left-invariant+1-forms"} left-invariant 1-forms
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+differential+forms"}> super differential forms
                ) on these spaces are
                %em not
                closed. This means that they carry natural intrinsic
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+of+a+G-structure"} torsion of a G-structure
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
                is richer than its bosonic counterpart in a way that goes beyond the addition of “superpartners”. For more on this see also at
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+constraints+of+supergravity"} torsion constraints of supergravity
            %li
              %p
                In particular
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-Minkowski+spacetimes"} super-Minkowski spacetimes
                carry non-trivial exceptional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super Lie algebra
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+cohomology"} cocycles
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definite+forms"} definite forms
                is hence
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analogy"} analogous
                to what is known in the bosonic case for instance for
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-structure"} G₂-structure
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+p-brane"} super p-brane
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-models"} sigma-models
                on curved
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
                backgrounds. Moreover, these cocycles classify
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+L-infinity+algebra"} super L-infinity algebra
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra+cohomology"} extensions
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+spacetime"} super Minkowski spacetime
                known as
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+super+Minkowski+spacetimes"} extended super Minkowski spacetimes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Cartan+geometry"} higher Cartan geometry
                within super-Cartan geometry.
          %h2#SurveyOfExistingLiterature Survey of (non-)existing literature
          %p
            While the specific terminology “super-Cartan geometry” is traditionally not used much (but see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Baaklini77a"} Baaklini 1977a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Baaklini77b"} 1977b
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#EgeilehChami13"} Egeileh &amp; Chami 2013
          %p
            Physics literature usually refers to the “superspace formulation” of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            when referring to the formulation of the theory in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            and uses terms such as “Einstein-Cartan theory” to refer to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+formulation+of+gravity"} first order formulation of gravity
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Nieuwenhuizen81"} Nieuwenhuizen 81
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            formulated this way in “superspace” is implicitly about super-Cartan geometry for the inclusion of a
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+group"} spin group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+cover"} double cover
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+group"} Lorentz group
            inside a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+group"} super Poincaré group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+gravity"} Einstein gravity
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+geometry"}> pseudo-Riemannian geometry
            ) is the Cartan geometry of the inclusion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+group"} Lorentz group
            inside a plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+group"} Poincaré group
            – this in fact being Cartan’s original and motivating example for the whole theory. Where physicists speak of “locally gauging
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergroup"} supergroup
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+group"} super Poincaré group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} acting
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+spacetime"} super Minkowski spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            that are locally (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+space"}> tangent space
            \-wise) modeled on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+space"} super Minkowski space
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+group"} Lorentz group
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-structure"} G-structure
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo-Riemannian metric
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundles"} principal bundles
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"} instantons
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anomalies"} anomalies
          %p
            With this understood, one physics references which explores the super-Cartan-geometric picture of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            in much detail is (
            %a{:href => "#DAuriaFre82"}> D’Auria-Fre 82
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#CastellaniDAuriaFre91"} Castellani-D’Auria-Fre 91
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Cartan+geometry"} higher Cartan geometry
            hidden here fairly explicit, see there and see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%27Auria-Fr%C3%A9+formulation+of+supergravity"} D'Auria-Fré formulation of supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein+geometry"} Klein geometry
            in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            is, even if not exactly in this terminology, rather explicit in (
            %a{:href => "#FigueroaOFarrill08"}> Figueroa-O’Farrill 08
            ).
          %p
            An early reference that identifies this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+formulation+of+gravity"} first order formulation of gravity
            explicitly as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+connection"} Cartan connection
            is (
            %a{:href => "#Baaklini77a"}> Baaklini 77a
            ,
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Baaklini77b"} Baaklini 77b
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Nieuwenhuizen81"} Nieuwenhuizen 81
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-structures"} G-structures
            (which is the relevant special class of super-Cartan geometry) in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+torsion+constraints"} supergravity torsion constraints
            is (
            %a{:href => "#Lott01"}> Lott 01
            ). The followup (
            %a{:href => "#EgeilehChami13"}> Egeileh-Chami 13
            ) to that article again makes the terminology “Cartan geometry” fully explicit in this supergeometric context. This last article also observes that from this perspective the traditional concept of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+spinor"} Killing spinor
            – which involves an extra “weakening” parameter in addition to the plain concept of a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariantly+constant+spinor"} covariantly constant spinor
            – is naturally understood as being in fact a covariantly constant spinor, but for a different model super-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein+geometry"} Klein geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/H
          %p
            This provides ample example and application of super-Cartan geometry for the case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/H
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+space"} super vector space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-structure"} G-structure
          %h2#Introduction Introduction
          %p
            It is traditional to introduce
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            as being about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifolds"} supermanifolds
            and to introduce the concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifolds"} supermanifolds
            in the form of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+ringed+topological+spaces"} locally ringed topological spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+sets"} geometry of physics – smooth sets
          %p Below in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#TheGeometricSubstance"} The geometric substance
          %p
            we consider the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            and find how its structure reflects the special qualities of supergeometry.
          %p Then in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#GStructureAndCartanGeometry"} G-Structure and Cartan geometry
          %p
            we discuss how to formulate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            generally in such a context. Finally in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#SuperCartanGeometry"} Super Cartan geometry
          %p we put this together and discuss supermanifolds equipped with super Cartan geometry.
          %h3#TheGeometricSubstance
            %strong 1)
            The geometric substance
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#CoordinareSystemsSuperCartesianSpaces"} Coordinate systems: super Cartesian spaces
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#TheGeometricDeterminations"} The geometric determinations
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#PrincipalBundlesAndHigherGeometry"} Principal bundles and Higher geometry
          %h4#CoordinareSystemsSuperCartesianSpaces Coordinate systems: super Cartesian spaces
          %p
            Recall the following from the discussion at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+sets"} geometry of physics – smooth sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            in direct analogy to this formulation of plain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+manifolds+and+orbifolds"} geometry of physics – manifolds and orbifolds
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+supergeometry"} geometry of physics – supergeometry
          #SiteCartSp.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Write
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in\mathbb{N}
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
              between them. Say that a collection of morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \to X\}
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CartSp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CartSp
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering"} covering
              if this is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+open+cover"} good open cover
              in that every finite non-empty intersection of the charts is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphic
              to a Cartesian space.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              We may think of this as the category of abstract
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+systems"} coordinate systems
              on which
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
              is to be modeled, see at
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+coordinate+systems"} geometry of physics – coordinate systems
          #Smooth0Type.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              We say a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+set"} smooth set
              or
              %em smooth 0-type
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi CartSp
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CartSp
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Smooth
                    %mn 0
                    %mi Type
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi CartSp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Smooth0Type \coloneqq Sh(CartSp)
            %p
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
              of all these.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              The useful way to think of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Smooth0Type"}
              in the present context is as defining a kind of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} generalized smooth space
              which is
              %em defined
              by which smooth functions from Cartesian spaces it receives (see also at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivation+for+sheaves%2C+cohomology+and+higher+stacks"} motivation for sheaves, cohomology and higher stacks
              for more exposition of this point).
            %p
              Under the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CartSp
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Smooth
                    %mn 0
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    CartSp \hookrightarrow Smooth0Type
            %p
              every Cartesian space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is naturally regarded as a smooth space itself, namely the one it
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} represents
              by the assignment
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ↦
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X \colon \mathbb{R}^n \mapsto C^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n ,X)
                    \\,.
            %p
              Hence the set that the Cartesian space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              is just the set of all ways of mapping that coordinate system smoothly into
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p
              Hence given any
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Smooth
                      %mn 0
                      %mi Type
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in Smooth0Type
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} generalized smooth space
              which need not be given as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              equipped with
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
              %em probe
              by mapping Cartesian spaces into it: given
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              then we think of the set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(\mathbb{R}^n)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              assigns, as playing the role of the set of all smooth functions “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n \longrightarrow X
              ” into the would-be space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
              gives that this is not circular, but consistent: once we identify Cartesian spaces themselves as smooth spaces via the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mi morphisms
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi of
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi smooth
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi spaces
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %mo }
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X(\mathbb{R}^n)
                    \\simeq
                    \\left\{
                    morphisms\;of\;smooth\;spaces\;
                    \\mathbb{R}^n \to X
                    \\right\}
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              The strategy is then to work with this nice category (a
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+spaces"} smooth spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategories"} subcategories
              of more specific objects having extra properties which one may need in given applications:
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
